From 53c05215912d725c72b0f069a82c111a0c8d537a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: miroe <260228444+boartat@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 02:40:58 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] dxmt: MUMA v1 - dynamic textures, time-based trim thread,
 purgeable spares

On top of MUMA v0:
- Dynamic linear textures (the path D3D11_USAGE_DYNAMIC 2D textures take)
  reclaim their spares too; both resource kinds share a DynamicSpareHolder
  base and each command queue's registry.
- A thread per command queue trims every 250 ms instead of the finish
  thread, so spares come back when the application stops submitting.
  A spare is freed once the GPU is done with it and it has waited 2 s.
- Spares of large Metal-allocated buffers are marked purgeable (volatile)
  as soon as the GPU is done with them and made non-volatile again on
  reuse: a discard never reads old contents, so the system may take the
  memory back at any time while the spare stays ready for reuse. Adds
  MTLResource_setPurgeableState to winemetal (unix call 146).
- Registration is lock-free once a holder is registered; the trim clears
  the flag under the holder's lock only when its queue is empty.
- MIROE_MUMA_FREE_MS and MIROE_MUMA_PURGEABLE=0 for measurements.

Engine/tests/c/d3d11-memory-test.c on ARM64 Wine + FEX, M4 Pro: after the
burst, buffers 920 -> 308 MB, an application that stops submitting 920 ->
308 MB (v0: 920), 64 MB dynamic textures 968 -> 200 MB (v0: 968). With
spares kept 60 s, purgeable spares leave 609 MB reclaimable and the
footprint at 417 MB instead of 1025 MB. Data written through reused spares
reads back exactly; burst and small-discard speed within noise of base.

Source: miroe (Miro project). License: LGPL-2.1-or-later, like DXMT.
Written with AI assistance: per DXMT's CONTRIBUTING this is not for an
upstream pull request.
